1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,154
A step that prints a title. It doesn't
build anything, it doesn't deploy anything,

2
00:00:09,194 --> 00:00:13,676
it doesn't touch a secret. A reviewer's
eye passes over it in a second, and the

3
00:00:13,716 --> 00:00:14,706
reviewer approves.

4
00:00:15,046 --> 00:00:19,691
That line is one of the planted weaknesses
of the lab, and its class began a real

5
00:00:19,731 --> 00:00:24,492
compromise: in the Nx incident, the first
step was a pull request title echoed in a

6
00:00:24,532 --> 00:00:29,292
privileged workflow. The textbook's root-cause
box describes what the team observes

7
00:00:29,332 --> 00:00:33,399
afterwards: a step that only prints
a title runs commands nobody wrote.

8
00:00:33,739 --> 00:00:39,109
To see why, you need one fact about timing
that you already know from video 143.

9
00:00:39,149 --> 00:00:43,979
Hold on to that one echo. You'll fix
it yourself before this video ends.

10
00:00:45,819 --> 00:00:49,468
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:49,508 --> 00:00:52,674
This is part four of the
model: expression injection.

12
00:00:53,014 --> 00:00:57,873
Three words first. A run step is one
entry of a workflow job: a shell command.

13
00:00:57,913 --> 00:01:02,396
The shell is the program that reads
text and carries it out as commands.

14
00:01:02,436 --> 00:01:07,547
And an expression is a formula, written
with a dollar sign and double braces, that

15
00:01:07,587 --> 00:01:09,997
GitHub Actions replaces with its value.

16
00:01:10,337 --> 00:01:15,394
You know the mechanism. In video 143 you
learned that an expression is evaluated

17
00:01:15,434 --> 00:01:20,555
before the shell starts, and that the
difference between an expression inside run

18
00:01:20,595 --> 00:01:23,995
and a value passed through
env is a security boundary.

19
00:01:24,035 --> 00:01:29,219
In video 144 you learned how the runner,
the machine that runs the job, executes a

20
00:01:29,259 --> 00:01:34,380
run step: it writes the text to a temporary
script file and starts a shell on it.

21
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:36,992
This video puts those two facts together.

22
00:01:37,332 --> 00:01:42,348
The video is defensive. You'll see the
unsafe step as GitHub's documentation prints

23
00:01:42,388 --> 00:01:47,159
it, and the documented fix, and a real
diff that applies the fix to a lab file.

24
00:01:47,199 --> 00:01:52,275
You won't see a crafted input. The
documentation's own demonstration is described in

25
00:01:52,315 --> 00:01:54,894
one sentence, as the
textbook describes it.

26
00:01:56,734 --> 00:01:58,313
Here's what you'll walk away with.

27
00:01:58,653 --> 00:02:00,065
After this video you can:

28
00:02:00,405 --> 00:02:05,802
explain why an expression inside run: is
code generation and not variable passing;

29
00:02:06,142 --> 00:02:08,517
list event fields an outsider controls;

30
00:02:08,857 --> 00:02:13,855
fix an injection by passing the value through
env and explain why the same value is

31
00:02:13,895 --> 00:02:14,705
then harmless;

32
00:02:15,045 --> 00:02:19,172
find candidate injections in a set
of workflows with a local search;

33
00:02:19,512 --> 00:02:22,365
write the review comment a
security engineer would write.

34
00:02:24,205 --> 00:02:29,147
In one sentence. An expression in dollar
sign and double braces is replaced by its

35
00:02:29,187 --> 00:02:34,251
value in the text of the script before the
shell starts, so a value that an outsider

36
00:02:34,291 --> 00:02:36,559
controls becomes part of your program.

37
00:02:36,899 --> 00:02:41,456
Precisely. The documentation: "The run
command executes within a temporary shell

38
00:02:41,496 --> 00:02:46,053
script on the runner. Before the shell
script is run, the expressions inside are

39
00:02:46,093 --> 00:02:50,362
evaluated and then substituted with the
resulting values, which can make it

40
00:02:50,402 --> 00:02:52,603
vulnerable to shell command injection."

41
00:02:52,943 --> 00:02:57,874
The order is the whole mechanism. Four
steps. One: GitHub Actions evaluates the

42
00:02:57,914 --> 00:03:01,523
expression. Two: it pastes the
value into the script text.

43
00:03:01,563 --> 00:03:05,425
Three: the runner writes that
text to a temporary script file.

44
00:03:05,465 --> 00:03:08,319
Four: the runner starts
the shell on the file.

45
00:03:08,659 --> 00:03:13,524
The shell never sees the expression. It sees
whatever the value contained, as source

46
00:03:13,564 --> 00:03:13,816
code.

47
00:03:14,156 --> 00:03:19,186
That's why the right phrase is code generation.
The workflow file is a template, and

48
00:03:19,226 --> 00:03:23,954
each run generates a new program from it.
If part of the input to the generator

49
00:03:23,994 --> 00:03:27,998
comes from an outsider, the outsider
is a co-author of the program.

50
00:03:28,338 --> 00:03:33,471
Which values are attacker-controlled? A
context is a named object of facts about a

51
00:03:33,511 --> 00:03:38,644
run, and the documentation says: "Attackers
can add their own malicious content to

52
00:03:38,684 --> 00:03:43,375
the github context, which should be
treated as potentially untrusted input.

53
00:03:43,415 --> 00:03:48,359
These contexts typically end with body,
default_branch, email, head_ref, label,

54
00:03:48,399 --> 00:03:51,008
message, name, page_name, ref, and title."

55
00:03:51,348 --> 00:03:56,259
Go through that list with Git in mind.
head_ref is a branch name, and Git allows

56
00:03:56,299 --> 00:03:59,848
characters in a branch name
that a shell treats as syntax.

57
00:03:59,888 --> 00:04:04,799
message is a commit message. name and
email are author fields, and you proved in

58
00:04:04,839 --> 00:04:09,873
video 138 that anyone can set those to
anything. Titles and bodies of issues, pull

59
00:04:09,913 --> 00:04:13,895
requests and comments are GitHub
data that any account can write.

60
00:04:14,235 --> 00:04:16,947
The unsafe step, as the
documentation prints it.

61
00:04:17,287 --> 00:04:21,621
Quick quiz. The author put the
expression in double quotes.

62
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:25,847
Does that make the step safe?
A, yes. B, no. Your answer?

63
00:04:30,687 --> 00:04:35,489
B. The quotes look careful, and they don't
help, because the value arrives before

64
00:04:35,529 --> 00:04:40,391
the shell parses anything. A title that
contains a double quote closes the string,

65
00:04:40,431 --> 00:04:45,173
and what follows it is parsed as commands.
The documentation's own demonstration

66
00:04:45,213 --> 00:04:49,297
uses a title that closes the quote
and lists the workspace directory.

67
00:04:49,337 --> 00:04:51,270
That's all I'll say about inputs.

68
00:04:51,610 --> 00:04:56,329
What an attacker achieves is arbitrary
commands in that job, with that job's token

69
00:04:56,369 --> 00:05:00,913
and secrets: its credential for the
repository, and the encrypted values it was

70
00:05:00,953 --> 00:05:01,262
given.

71
00:05:01,602 --> 00:05:06,328
Where that matters. On pull_request from
a fork, a second repository made from

72
00:05:06,368 --> 00:05:11,094
yours, it's contained by the fork model
of the last video: read-only token, no

73
00:05:11,134 --> 00:05:15,861
secrets. On pull_request_target, issues,
issue_comment or discussion, it isn't

74
00:05:15,901 --> 00:05:19,161
contained. Those run with
the base repository's token.

75
00:05:19,501 --> 00:05:23,824
The fixed step, with the documented
mitigation, an intermediate environment

76
00:05:23,864 --> 00:05:28,595
variable. An environment variable is a
named value that a program receives when it

77
00:05:28,635 --> 00:05:29,002
starts.

78
00:05:35,142 --> 00:05:38,616
Why this form is safe. The
expression is still evaluated.

79
00:05:38,656 --> 00:05:43,794
But its value no longer goes into the script
text. In the documentation's words, the

80
00:05:43,834 --> 00:05:48,726
value "is stored in memory and used as a
variable, and doesn't interact with the

81
00:05:48,766 --> 00:05:50,390
script generation process".

82
00:05:50,730 --> 00:05:55,280
Follow the consequences. The script that
the shell parses is now a constant.

83
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:59,267
It's identical for every pull
request, and you can review it once.

84
00:05:59,307 --> 00:06:03,977
The title reaches the shell as the value
of a variable, after parsing is over.

85
00:06:04,017 --> 00:06:08,930
And the shell doesn't re-parse the value
of a variable as commands when it expands

86
00:06:08,970 --> 00:06:09,111
it.

87
00:06:09,451 --> 00:06:13,887
The double quotes around the variable
are still needed, for the ordinary shell

88
00:06:13,927 --> 00:06:18,477
reason: an unquoted expansion is split
into words and glob-expanded, meaning its

89
00:06:18,517 --> 00:06:21,404
wildcard characters are
matched against file names.

90
00:06:21,444 --> 00:06:24,445
The documentation tells you
to quote for that reason.

91
00:06:24,785 --> 00:06:29,424
A second mitigation, which the same reference
prefers when available: use an action

92
00:06:29,464 --> 00:06:32,862
instead of an inline script, and
pass the value through with.

93
00:06:33,202 --> 00:06:35,666
Two notes for review,
both from the textbook.

94
00:06:36,006 --> 00:06:40,391
The rule is about where the value
lands, not which context it comes from.

95
00:06:40,431 --> 00:06:45,482
A pull request number can't carry code. But
a reviewer who decides case by case will

96
00:06:45,522 --> 00:06:50,392
eventually decide wrongly. So many teams
adopt the mechanical rule: no expression

97
00:06:50,432 --> 00:06:55,422
inside run at all. The course workflows
follow it, including for values that aren't

98
00:06:55,462 --> 00:06:56,635
attacker-controlled.

99
00:06:56,975 --> 00:07:01,848
And env doesn't make later misuse safe.
Passing the variable to eval, the shell

100
00:07:01,888 --> 00:07:06,388
command that runs a string as code,
or writing the value unvalidated into

101
00:07:06,428 --> 00:07:09,560
GITHUB_ENV, brings the
problem back one layer down.

102
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:16,634
Analogy, from the textbook. Mail merge. You
write "Dear, name, your order shipped",

103
00:07:16,674 --> 00:07:21,971
and the software pastes the name in. If the
letter is then read aloud by someone who

104
00:07:22,011 --> 00:07:27,117
obeys every sentence, a customer whose
"name" contains a sentence gets it obeyed.

105
00:07:27,457 --> 00:07:32,310
The textbook says the analogy is close,
and breaks only in one respect: a shell is

106
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:37,144
far more obedient than any reader, and
treats quotes and separators in the pasted

107
00:07:37,184 --> 00:07:38,218
text as structure.

108
00:07:38,558 --> 00:07:43,010
Now the two forms in that picture. With
the expression inside run, the name is

109
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:46,235
pasted into the letter before
it's handed to the reader.

110
00:07:46,275 --> 00:07:50,727
The reader can't tell which words were
yours and which came from the customer.

111
00:07:50,767 --> 00:07:55,219
With env, the letter says "read out the
content of the envelope marked TITLE".

112
00:07:55,259 --> 00:07:59,941
The letter is always the same. The envelope's
content is read out as a name, never

113
00:07:59,981 --> 00:08:01,151
obeyed as a sentence.

114
00:08:01,491 --> 00:08:05,829
That's the difference between code and
data, and the whole fix is to keep the

115
00:08:05,869 --> 00:08:07,705
outsider's text on the data side.

116
00:08:09,545 --> 00:08:14,281
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Write the four steps in order, from the

117
00:08:14,321 --> 00:08:19,119
workflow file to the running shell, and
mark the step where an outsider's text

118
00:08:19,159 --> 00:08:20,607
enters. Say it out loud.

119
00:08:25,447 --> 00:08:29,594
Check your order. Evaluate, paste,
write the file, start the shell.

120
00:08:29,634 --> 00:08:34,594
The outsider's text enters at step two,
and the shell only arrives at step four.

121
00:08:34,634 --> 00:08:39,594
So the shell never sees the expression.
It sees whatever the value contained, as

122
00:08:39,634 --> 00:08:40,343
source code.

123
00:08:49,383 --> 00:08:56,603
Step 1: find the candidate. One snippet
from labs/run ch21a/lab-29-1-inventory,

124
00:08:56,643 --> 00:09:00,555
which you saw at the
end of the last video.

125
00:09:00,895 --> 00:09:04,704
One line, in file 1, line 32,
inside a multi-line script.

126
00:09:04,744 --> 00:09:10,241
There's the echo from the opening. Two
expressions on it: the issue number and the

127
00:09:10,281 --> 00:09:15,846
issue title. Classify each. Which of the
two can carry text that an outsider wrote?

128
00:09:15,886 --> 00:09:20,978
And which trigger did file 1 have: is
this job in the contained lane or not?

129
00:09:21,018 --> 00:09:22,059
Say it out loud.

130
00:09:26,899 --> 00:09:31,227
The number can't carry code. The
title is on the documentation's list.

131
00:09:31,267 --> 00:09:36,219
And file 1 runs on an opened issue, with
the base repository's token and issues:

132
00:09:36,259 --> 00:09:41,398
write. So: an outsider's text, placed where
the shell expects source code, in a job

133
00:09:41,438 --> 00:09:42,584
with a write scope.

134
00:09:42,924 --> 00:09:47,617
Step 2: the fix. Replay
labs/run ch21a/lab-29-1-fixes.

135
00:09:47,657 --> 00:09:54,541
It applies the reference fix to each of
the five lab files and shows the diffs.

136
00:09:54,881 --> 00:09:59,509
A word before it runs. The replay
contains the fixes for all five files.

137
00:09:59,549 --> 00:10:03,983
Four of them belong to other videos
and to your own work in Lab 29.1.

138
00:10:04,023 --> 00:10:08,262
This video shows the summary and
the one diff that is its subject.

139
00:10:08,302 --> 00:10:12,087
If you haven't done the lab
yet, don't read the other four.

140
00:10:12,427 --> 00:10:15,865
Five files changed, 24
insertions and 33 deletions.

141
00:10:15,905 --> 00:10:21,252
The fixes remove more than they add.
That's typical: securing a workflow mostly

142
00:10:21,292 --> 00:10:22,956
means taking things away.

143
00:10:23,296 --> 00:10:28,381
Before the diff appears, write the fix
yourself. You need three new lines above run,

144
00:10:28,421 --> 00:10:32,102
an env line and two variables,
and one changed line below it.

145
00:10:32,142 --> 00:10:33,932
Make your prediction on paper.

146
00:10:38,772 --> 00:10:43,667
Read the second hunk. Three added lines: an
env mapping with two variables, each set

147
00:10:43,707 --> 00:10:48,249
from an expression. One removed line and
one added line in the script: the two

148
00:10:48,289 --> 00:10:52,068
expressions are gone, and in their
place are two shell variables.

149
00:10:52,408 --> 00:10:56,942
Now apply the test from the concept
section. Is the script text a constant?

150
00:10:56,982 --> 00:11:01,028
Yes: it's the same for every issue.
Where do the two values arrive?

151
00:11:01,068 --> 00:11:06,151
In the environment, after parsing. The number
was moved too, although it can't carry

152
00:11:06,191 --> 00:11:11,030
code. That's the mechanical rule: no
expression inside run, without case-by-case

153
00:11:11,070 --> 00:11:11,579
judgment.

154
00:11:11,919 --> 00:11:14,204
Step 3: verify with the scans.

155
00:11:14,544 --> 00:11:19,119
Four scans, and each must come back
empty. The third is the awk program for

156
00:11:19,159 --> 00:11:23,919
multi-line scripts, and it prints nothing.
The fourth is the single-line form.

157
00:11:23,959 --> 00:11:28,720
The other two belong to the last video
and the next: no privileged trigger, no

158
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:33,704
opt-out flag, no write-all, and no action
reference that isn't a 40-character ID.

159
00:11:34,044 --> 00:11:38,967
Every file declares permissions once.
And the one secret is named in one step.

160
00:11:39,307 --> 00:11:42,935
git commit is 🟢 SAFE: it adds
an object and moves the branch.

161
00:11:42,975 --> 00:11:47,987
The status is clean. In your own run of the
lab you make one commit per file, with a

162
00:11:48,027 --> 00:11:49,972
message that says what you fixed.

163
00:11:50,312 --> 00:11:55,334
The review comment. Here's the form a security
engineer would use for that line, and

164
00:11:55,374 --> 00:12:00,275
you can adapt it. Name the line. Name the
class: script injection through an event

165
00:12:00,315 --> 00:12:05,337
field. State what is substituted and when:
the issue title is pasted into the script

166
00:12:05,377 --> 00:12:07,085
text before the shell starts.

167
00:12:07,425 --> 00:12:12,311
State the condition: the workflow runs on
issues, so anyone who can open an issue

168
00:12:12,351 --> 00:12:15,777
supplies that text, and the
job's token can write issues.

169
00:12:15,817 --> 00:12:20,643
State the smallest change: move both
expressions to env and reference the quoted

170
00:12:20,683 --> 00:12:24,656
variables. And cite the documentation's
page on script injections.

171
00:12:24,696 --> 00:12:29,096
Five sentences, no drama, and the
author can act on it without a meeting.

172
00:12:30,936 --> 00:12:32,584
Five mistakes to watch for.

173
00:12:32,924 --> 00:12:36,218
Believing that quotes around
the expression make it safe.

174
00:12:36,258 --> 00:12:40,664
Root cause: the value is pasted into
the script text before the shell parses

175
00:12:40,704 --> 00:12:44,232
anything, so a quote character
in the value ends your string.

176
00:12:44,572 --> 00:12:48,377
Scanning only lines that contain
both run: and an expression.

177
00:12:48,417 --> 00:12:53,609
Root cause: in a multi-line script the
expression is on a later line than run:, and

178
00:12:53,649 --> 00:12:55,626
the narrow scan returns nothing.

179
00:12:55,966 --> 00:13:00,675
Deciding case by case which contexts are
harmless. Root cause: the rule is about

180
00:13:00,715 --> 00:13:05,186
where the value lands; a reviewer who
judges each case will eventually judge

181
00:13:05,226 --> 00:13:09,044
wrongly, which is why teams forbid
expressions in run altogether.

182
00:13:09,384 --> 00:13:12,970
Moving the value to env and
then using it as code anyway.

183
00:13:13,010 --> 00:13:18,251
Root cause: eval on the variable, or writing
it unvalidated into GITHUB_ENV, brings

184
00:13:18,291 --> 00:13:20,287
the problem back one layer down.

185
00:13:20,627 --> 00:13:25,825
Leaving the variable unquoted. Root cause:
an unquoted expansion is split into words

186
00:13:25,865 --> 00:13:26,948
and glob-expanded.

187
00:13:28,788 --> 00:13:33,871
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's case.
A prompt-evaluation workflow prints the

188
00:13:33,911 --> 00:13:38,740
pull request title in a job that holds
a provider API key, because it runs on

189
00:13:38,780 --> 00:13:43,610
pull_request_target. The textbook's
comment: that one line is the Nx pattern.

190
00:13:43,950 --> 00:13:47,275
The fix is two edits, and they're
from two different videos.

191
00:13:47,315 --> 00:13:50,360
The env form, from this one,
so that the title is data.

192
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:55,071
And removing the privileged trigger, from
the last one, so that the key isn't in the

193
00:13:55,111 --> 00:13:55,688
job at all.

194
00:13:56,028 --> 00:13:59,348
Notice that either edit alone
would have left something.

195
00:13:59,388 --> 00:14:04,387
With only the first, an outsider's pull
request still starts a job that holds a key.

196
00:14:04,427 --> 00:14:09,427
With only the second, the injectable line
is still in the file, waiting for the next

197
00:14:09,467 --> 00:14:14,106
person who changes the trigger. A review
that finds one weakness in a workflow

198
00:14:14,146 --> 00:14:15,966
should finish reading the file.

